WEBVTT

00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:04.142
A running total is another common window-function pattern.

00:00:04.142 --> 00:00:09.937
Instead of collapsing sales by date, we can keep each date or row in sequence while showing the cumulative

00:00:09.937 --> 00:00:12.507
revenue up to that point.

00:00:12.507 --> 00:00:17.832
The ORDER BY inside the window defines the progression of the calculation.

00:00:17.832 --> 00:00:21.707
Run the example and watch the running total increase as the dates move forward.

00:00:21.707 --> 00:00:27.977
This pattern appears in financial balances, cumulative enrollment, inventory movement, project progress,

00:00:27.977 --> 00:00:29.252
and many other time-ordered analyses.

00:00:29.252 --> 00:00:33.982
When you build a running total, make sure the ordering is deterministic.

00:00:33.982 --> 00:00:40.122
If multiple rows can share the same date, think about whether you need an additional ordering column to define

00:00:40.122 --> 00:00:46.922
a stable sequence. Window functions calculate across related rows without collapsing them.

00:00:46.922 --> 00:00:50.722
That lets you keep row-level detail while adding rank, running totals, or group averages.

00:00:50.722 --> 00:00:58.307
Always define the partition and ordering carefully because those clauses describe which rows belong together

00:00:58.307 --> 00:00:59.360
and in what sequence.
